("place.memory", "isa", "Place")
("place.memory", "slug", "memory")
("place.memory", "route", "places/memory.html")
("place.memory", "title", "Gone from the picture. Still in the motion.")
("place.memory", "lead", "Hiding part of a system does not necessarily stop it affecting the rest.")
("place.memory", "region", "The echo chamber")
("place.memory", "chapter", "22")
("place.memory", "source", "note.memory")
("place.memory", "light", "unseen")
("place.memory", "paragraph", "The example has two changing quantities that affect one another. Hide one line: the other line keeps the same path. Hiding the line changed the picture, not the calculation.")
("place.memory", "paragraph", "Now disconnect the link between them. This changes the calculation itself. The example helps explain how an unseen part can leave an effect over time. Some hidden parts leave no detectable effect; the next room explores that limit.")
("place.memory", "fullParagraph", "The finite linear result begins with visible and hidden coordinates that can affect one another. Solve out the hidden part and it can return as dependence on earlier visible states and on a hidden initial condition. The reduced equation remembers what its state list no longer displays.")
("place.memory", "fullParagraph", "The small instrument here integrates two coupled coordinates. You can hide one trace while the visible evolution still depends on both. Removing the coupling is a different intervention. These operations change different things.")
("place.memory", "fullParagraph", "This is a mathematical lesson with a boundary. Some hidden sectors are exactly invisible and dynamically decoupled from the record. Their absence does not force an accessible echo. The next room keeps that harder limit in view.")
("place.memory", "word", "word.model")
("place.memory", "word", "word.coupling")
("place.memory", "pause", "What remains when something disappears?")
("place.memory", "instruction", "First hide a line. Then disconnect the link. Compare what each action changes.")
("place.memory", "choice", "place.memory.choice.0")
("place.memory.choice.0", "destination", "place.erasure")
("place.memory.choice.0", "label", "What if no echo survives?")
("place.memory.choice.0", "kind", "Mathematical limit")
("place.memory", "choice", "place.memory.choice.1")
("place.memory.choice.1", "destination", "place.preservation")
("place.memory.choice.1", "label", "Follow a trace through time")
("place.memory.choice.1", "kind", "Ideas to compare")
("place.memory", "choice", "place.memory.choice.2")
("place.memory.choice.2", "destination", "place.return")
("place.memory.choice.2", "label", "Come back changed")
("place.memory.choice.2", "kind", "Related mathematical question")
("place.erasure", "isa", "Place")
("place.erasure", "slug", "erasure")
("place.erasure", "route", "places/erasure.html")
("place.erasure", "title", "A record can forget completely.")
("place.erasure", "lead", "Once a record leaves out a difference, that record may be unable to recover it.")
("place.erasure", "region", "The erased distinction")
("place.erasure", "chapter", "23")
("place.erasure", "source", "note.erasure")
("place.erasure", "light", "unseen")
("place.erasure", "paragraph", "The pairs 1 and 3, or 2 and 2, both add up to 4. If the only record is the sum, you cannot tell which pair it came from.")
("place.erasure", "paragraph", "Keep the first number as a second record and you can tell these pairs apart. That uses new information. It does not recover the answer from the sum alone.")
("place.erasure", "fullParagraph", "Take two labelled pairs: one and three, or two and two. A detector that keeps only their sum reports four for both. You cannot tell which pair occurred from that sum alone. Repeating the same arithmetic does not recover the missing label.")
("place.erasure", "fullParagraph", "Add a second record that retains the first coordinate and the pair becomes identifiable in this tiny declared family. You have enriched the record; you have not extracted the answer from the original sum.")
("place.erasure", "fullParagraph", "The corpus’s recovery boundary is strict: not every reduction leaves a recoverable remnant of everything it removes. A truthful account can finish by naming what the available theory cannot tell you. That limit is useful knowledge.")
("place.erasure", "word", "word.record")
("place.erasure", "pause", "What remains when something disappears?")
("place.erasure", "instruction", "Try both pairs. Then keep a second record and see what becomes clear.")
("place.erasure", "choice", "place.erasure.choice.0")
("place.erasure.choice.0", "destination", "place.memory")
("place.erasure.choice.0", "label", "Return to a surviving influence")
("place.erasure.choice.0", "kind", "Another way back")
("place.erasure", "choice", "place.erasure.choice.1")
("place.erasure.choice.1", "destination", "place.observation")
("place.erasure.choice.1", "label", "Ask the detector another question")
("place.erasure.choice.1", "kind", "Ideas to compare")
("place.erasure", "choice", "place.erasure.choice.2")
("place.erasure.choice.2", "destination", "place.closedpath")
("place.erasure.choice.2", "label", "Keep the limit on the map")
("place.erasure.choice.2", "kind", "Ideas to compare")
("place.attention", "isa", "Place")
("place.attention", "slug", "attention")
("place.attention", "route", "places/attention.html")
("place.attention", "title", "The same clearing. A different field of attention.")
("place.attention", "lead", "Looking closely at one thing can make its surroundings harder to notice.")
("place.attention", "region", "The living canopy")
("place.attention", "chapter", "24")
("place.attention", "source", "note.mind")
("place.attention", "light", "human")
("place.attention", "paragraph", "Try the two views of the clearing. One keeps several details visible. The other brings the leaf forward while the rest recedes.")
("place.attention", "paragraph", "This changes the drawing; it does not test your attention. The research separates noticing, choosing, awareness and action because explaining one does not automatically explain the others.")
("place.attention", "fullParagraph", "Change the canopy’s view. In one setting, one cluster is sharply available and the rest recedes. In another, the wider arrangement becomes legible. The illustration changes presentation; it does not measure your attention or assign you a type.")
("place.attention", "fullParagraph", "The functional atlas distinguishes readiness, sustained processing, vigilance, selective focus and divided allocation. It also separates these from conscious access, action and the experience of agency. A familiar word can compress several different operations.")
("place.attention", "fullParagraph", "The useful question is which transition is being explained, on which evidence, and what rival account remains. A diagram of a distributed system cannot by itself tell us what experience is. That unresolved join belongs in the clearing too.")
("place.attention", "word", "word.conscious access")
("place.attention", "pause", "What comes into attention?")
("place.attention", "instruction", "Try the broad view and the close view. Notice what each brings forward.")
("place.attention", "choice", "place.attention.choice.0")
("place.attention.choice.0", "destination", "place.will")
("place.attention.choice.0", "label", "Follow an intention toward action")
("place.attention.choice.0", "kind", "Ideas to compare")
("place.attention", "choice", "place.attention.choice.1")
("place.attention.choice.1", "destination", "place.listening")
("place.attention.choice.1", "label", "Turn toward another person")
("place.attention.choice.1", "kind", "Ideas to compare")
("place.attention", "choice", "place.attention.choice.2")
("place.attention.choice.2", "destination", "place.stone")
("place.attention.choice.2", "label", "Look closely at an ordinary thing")
("place.attention.choice.2", "kind", "Ideas to compare")
("place.will", "isa", "Place")
("place.will", "slug", "will")
("place.will", "route", "places/will.html")
("place.will", "title", "An intention is not yet an action.")
("place.will", "lead", "Choosing to act and carrying out an action are different parts of a story.")
("place.will", "region", "The unfinished intention")
("place.will", "chapter", "25")
("place.will", "source", "note.relations")
("place.will", "light", "human")
("place.will", "paragraph", "Open the links between getting ready, choosing, acting and receiving feedback. Each link raises a question about what connects the steps.")
("place.will", "paragraph", "A neat diagram can hide a gap in our understanding. The research marks uncertain or missing connections instead of treating every arrow as an established explanation of the brain.")
("place.will", "fullParagraph", "The relationship-first atlas treats connections as conditional objects. A relation names its source, target, needed inputs, evidence and limits. A possible sequence on paper is not proof that every link is present in a particular biological system.")
("place.will", "fullParagraph", "In this small sequence, readiness, selection, action and feedback each have a role. Open a join to see the question it carries. There is no score for how your own life follows the diagram; the display makes no observation of you.")
("place.will", "fullParagraph", "Negative and unknown relations matter as much as proposed positive ones. They prevent a smooth drawing from claiming a completed explanation. Asking where a process stops being understood can be a way of taking agency seriously.")
("place.will", "word", "word.feedback")
("place.will", "word", "word.agency")
("place.will", "pause", "What comes into attention?")
("place.will", "instruction", "Open each link to see what would be needed to explain it.")
("place.will", "choice", "place.will.choice.0")
("place.will.choice.0", "destination", "place.attention")
("place.will.choice.0", "label", "Return to the canopy")
("place.will.choice.0", "kind", "Another way back")
("place.will", "choice", "place.will.choice.1")
("place.will.choice.1", "destination", "place.coexistence")
("place.will.choice.1", "label", "Let intentions meet constraints")
("place.will.choice.1", "kind", "Ideas to compare")
("place.will", "choice", "place.will.choice.2")
("place.will.choice.2", "destination", "place.commons")
("place.will.choice.2", "label", "Invite another account")
("place.will.choice.2", "kind", "Ideas to compare")
("place.stone", "isa", "Place")
("place.stone", "slug", "stone")
("place.stone", "route", "places/stone.html")
("place.stone", "title", "One stone. Many histories.")
("place.stone", "lead", "A stone can tell several stories, and each needs a different kind of evidence.")
("place.stone", "region", "The house of stone")
("place.stone", "chapter", "26")
("place.stone", "source", "note.material")
("place.stone", "light", "human")
("place.stone", "paragraph", "Its minerals can help identify the material. Marks may help us ask how it was worked. Its location and surroundings can help us place it in a history.")
("place.stone", "paragraph", "Try those three views. The drawing is not a measured sample. It introduces a research approach that uses the pyramids to connect materials, making, preservation and historical evidence.")
("place.stone", "fullParagraph", "A stone in a monument is material, worked object and surviving record. Mineral assemblage, fracture, abrasion, transport, assembly and exposure constrain different parts of its history. None of those views is sufficient simply because it is detailed.")
("place.stone", "fullParagraph", "The material-history programme uses the pyramids as its named entry. It asks what sequences of selection, working, movement and preservation could leave the observed traces. Its register proposes research joins; it does not settle a monument’s history by drawing an attractive physical analogy.")
("place.stone", "fullParagraph", "The stone here is an illustration, not a measured sample. Move among its lenses: composition, working and context. Notice the different evidence each would need. A good question can cross scales without pretending to calculate an entire pyramid from first principles.")
("place.stone", "word", "word.provenance")
("place.stone", "pause", "How did this come to be here?")
("place.stone", "instruction", "Choose a view of the stone. Each asks for a different kind of evidence.")
("place.stone", "choice", "place.stone.choice.0")
("place.stone.choice.0", "destination", "place.preservation")
("place.stone.choice.0", "label", "Ask why this trace survived")
("place.stone.choice.0", "kind", "Ideas to compare")
("place.stone", "choice", "place.stone.choice.1")
("place.stone.choice.1", "destination", "place.tiling")
("place.stone.choice.1", "label", "Walk toward the patterned wall")
("place.stone.choice.1", "kind", "Ideas to compare")
("place.stone", "choice", "place.stone.choice.2")
