("place.stone.choice.2", "label", "Bring an instrument to the question")
("place.stone.choice.2", "kind", "Ideas to compare")
("place.preservation", "isa", "Place")
("place.preservation", "slug", "preservation")
("place.preservation", "route", "places/preservation.html")
("place.preservation", "title", "What survives is not everything that happened.")
("place.preservation", "lead", "The evidence left today is only part of what happened.")
("place.preservation", "region", "The surviving trace")
("place.preservation", "chapter", "27")
("place.preservation", "source", "note.material")
("place.preservation", "light", "human")
("place.preservation", "paragraph", "A mark may be absent because a process never happened, because the mark was damaged later, or because the measuring tool missed it.")
("place.preservation", "paragraph", "Choose an explanation to see what it would need. Weathering, burial and later care can all change which traces survive. A claim about the past needs to account for those changes too.")
("place.preservation", "fullParagraph", "Exposure, burial, salts, disturbance and conservation can change which traces remain available. A missing mark may reflect an absent process, later loss, or a detector that cannot resolve it. Those explanations require different checks.")
("place.preservation", "fullParagraph", "The material-history programme therefore places preservation between transformation and present observation. An explanation should predict both the proposed working process and the distribution of evidence that could survive it.")
("place.preservation", "fullParagraph", "Try the record layers on the illustrative stone. The labels are questions for an investigation, not reconstructions of an actual sampled object. Holding that distinction lets curiosity remain generous while claims stay bounded.")
("place.preservation", "word", "word.preservation")
("place.preservation", "pause", "How did this come to be here?")
("place.preservation", "instruction", "Choose a reason a trace might be missing. Read what would need checking.")
("place.preservation", "choice", "place.preservation.choice.0")
("place.preservation.choice.0", "destination", "place.stone")
("place.preservation.choice.0", "label", "Return to the object")
("place.preservation.choice.0", "kind", "Another way back")
("place.preservation", "choice", "place.preservation.choice.1")
("place.preservation.choice.1", "destination", "place.erasure")
("place.preservation.choice.1", "label", "Ask what the record cannot recover")
("place.preservation.choice.1", "kind", "Ideas to compare")
("place.preservation", "choice", "place.preservation.choice.2")
("place.preservation.choice.2", "destination", "place.texts")
("place.preservation.choice.2", "label", "Enter the reading room")
("place.preservation.choice.2", "kind", "Optional historical crossing")
("place.tiling", "isa", "Place")
("place.tiling", "slug", "tiling")
("place.tiling", "route", "places/tiling.html")
("place.tiling", "title", "Grow a pattern. Keep count.")
("place.tiling", "lead", "A precise counting rule can connect patterns without making them the same thing.")
("place.tiling", "region", "The patterned wall")
("place.tiling", "chapter", "28")
("place.tiling", "source", "note.patterns")
("place.tiling", "light", "unseen")
("place.tiling", "paragraph", "Begin with the pair 1, 1. Each press applies the same rule: the first new number is twice the first old number plus the second; the second new number is the sum of the old pair.")
("place.tiling", "paragraph", "The research connects this rule to particular tiling patterns through several mathematical steps. The ornament here is decorative. The counter shows the exact small calculation.")
("place.tiling", "fullParagraph", "Start with the pair one, one. The declared action sends a pair to two times the first plus the second, and then the first plus the second. Each press applies the same integer matrix. Its counts grow while a precise relation remains available to inspect.")
("place.tiling", "fullParagraph", "The source connects this two-state Fibonacci/Penrose action to a particular girih count construction through a quotient, normalization and lattice embedding. Those intermediate operations carry the evidence. Sharing a golden number would not supply them.")
("place.tiling", "fullParagraph", "The rosette is decorative geometry, not a reconstruction of the historical artwork. The counter is the exact small calculation. Keeping ornament and result distinct allows both to be enjoyed on their own terms.")
("place.tiling", "word", "word.transformation")
("place.tiling", "word", "word.carrier")
("place.tiling", "pause", "What changes while the pattern holds?")
("place.tiling", "instruction", "Apply the rule one step at a time. Watch the two numbers. Reset to begin again.")
("place.tiling", "choice", "place.tiling.choice.0")
("place.tiling.choice.0", "destination", "place.code")
("place.tiling.choice.0", "label", "Ask what a pattern can repair")
("place.tiling.choice.0", "kind", "Ideas to compare")
("place.tiling", "choice", "place.tiling.choice.1")
("place.tiling.choice.1", "destination", "place.stone")
("place.tiling.choice.1", "label", "Return to its material setting")
("place.tiling.choice.1", "kind", "Ideas to compare")
("place.tiling", "choice", "place.tiling.choice.2")
("place.tiling.choice.2", "destination", "place.monster")
("place.tiling.choice.2", "label", "Visit the exceptional cabinet")
("place.tiling.choice.2", "kind", "Ideas to compare")
("place.code", "isa", "Place")
("place.code", "slug", "code")
("place.code", "route", "places/code.html")
("place.code", "title", "Can a message survive a mistake?")
("place.code", "lead", "Repeating a message can help repair a mistake, within a known limit.")
("place.code", "region", "The repair bench")
("place.code", "chapter", "29")
("place.code", "source", "note.codes")
("place.code", "light", "unseen")
("place.code", "paragraph", "To send zero, use 000. To send one, use 111. The reader of the message chooses whichever bit appears most often.")
("place.code", "paragraph", "Choose a starting bit, then flip one transmitted bit. The original is recovered. Flip two and the answer changes to the wrong original. This small example is different from the larger error-correcting code discussed in the research.")
("place.code", "fullParagraph", "This bench uses a deliberately small repetition code. The bit zero is represented by three zeros, and one by three ones. A majority decoder repairs one flipped bit. With two flips it confidently returns the wrong original bit.")
("place.code", "fullParagraph", "That is the whole declared example. It explains why redundancy, allowed words, distance and error assumptions matter. It is not the ternary Golay code studied in the source, and it supplies no bridge from a code to a physical or biological mechanism.")
("place.code", "fullParagraph", "The source’s arithmetic audit also retains an obstruction: a proposed intrinsic ordering does not follow from the named symmetry. A successful calculation and a failed promotion can live together in the same workshop.")
("place.code", "word", "word.code")
("place.code", "word", "word.bit")
("place.code", "pause", "What changes while the pattern holds?")
("place.code", "instruction", "Choose a starting bit: zero or one. Flip one of the three bits, then a second. Watch the decoded answer.")
("place.code", "choice", "place.code.choice.0")
("place.code.choice.0", "destination", "place.tiling")
("place.code.choice.0", "label", "Return to the transformation")
("place.code.choice.0", "kind", "Another way back")
("place.code", "choice", "place.code.choice.1")
("place.code.choice.1", "destination", "place.closedpath")
("place.code.choice.1", "label", "See why a proposed step failed")
("place.code.choice.1", "kind", "Ideas to compare")
("place.code", "choice", "place.code.choice.2")
("place.code.choice.2", "destination", "place.representation")
("place.code.choice.2", "label", "Lift the surface of a model")
("place.code.choice.2", "kind", "Ideas to compare")
("place.laboratory", "isa", "Place")
("place.laboratory", "slug", "laboratory")
("place.laboratory", "route", "places/laboratory.html")
("place.laboratory", "title", "How could we tell the explanations apart?")
("place.laboratory", "lead", "Choose a measurement that could tell competing explanations apart.")
("place.laboratory", "region", "The working laboratory")
("place.laboratory", "chapter", "30")
("place.laboratory", "source", "note.laboratory")
("place.laboratory", "light", "unseen")
("place.laboratory", "paragraph", "Two explanations may agree on one reading and disagree on another. A timing reference, a reversed path or a different waiting time can help reveal the difference.")
("place.laboratory", "paragraph", "Choose a check at the bench. These are proposed ways to test ideas, not measurements being collected here. Better tools can be useful even when the result agrees with existing physics.")
("place.laboratory", "fullParagraph", "The finesse pathway proposes controlled carriers, coherent transformations, independent references, multichannel records and adversarial inference. Its practical value is not conditional on discovering an anomaly. Better sensing and clearer mechanism identification can remain useful conventional results.")
("place.laboratory", "fullParagraph", "Choose a bench question. A reversible path, an added phase reference or a changed waiting time can expose different collisions between explanations. The proposed controls do not all answer the same question, and none is a substitute for a calibrated apparatus.")
("place.laboratory", "fullParagraph", "This is a design encounter. No acquisition runs behind the glass. The next evidence would include the physical carrier, nuisance envelope, raw records and a decision rule fixed before opening the result.")
("place.laboratory", "word", "word.control")
("place.laboratory", "word", "word.phase")
("place.laboratory", "pause", "What would make the explanations disagree?")
("place.laboratory", "instruction", "Choose a comparison to see which difference it could reveal.")
("place.laboratory", "choice", "place.laboratory.choice.0")
("place.laboratory.choice.0", "destination", "place.cost")
("place.laboratory.choice.0", "label", "Ask what the control costs")
("place.laboratory.choice.0", "kind", "Ideas to compare")
("place.laboratory", "choice", "place.laboratory.choice.1")
("place.laboratory.choice.1", "destination", "place.cosmos")
("place.laboratory.choice.1", "label", "Walk toward the observatories")
("place.laboratory.choice.1", "kind", "Ideas to compare")
("place.laboratory", "choice", "place.laboratory.choice.2")
("place.laboratory.choice.2", "destination", "place.replay")
("place.laboratory.choice.2", "label", "Keep the record inspectable")
("place.laboratory.choice.2", "kind", "Ideas to compare")
("place.cost", "isa", "Place")
("place.cost", "slug", "cost")
("place.cost", "route", "places/cost.html")
("place.cost", "title", "Slower can cost more.")
("place.cost", "lead", "Improving one part of a result can make another part worse.")
("place.cost", "region", "The control ledger")
("place.cost", "chapter", "31")
("place.cost", "source", "note.instrument")
("place.cost", "light", "unseen")
("place.cost", "paragraph", "In the reported computer experiment, slower movement reduces one kind of error but allows more of the signal to fade away. The best overall return among the four tested settings occurs at an intermediate speed.")
("place.cost", "paragraph", "Choose a setting and compare both numbers. These are saved results from that computer model. They are not measurements from a physical device or a best setting for every device.")
("place.cost", "fullParagraph", "The documented four-mode numerical experiment compares several loop rates. In its metered example, slowing the loop suppresses bright leakage while accumulated dissipation rises. The raw target overlap peaks at one of the tested intermediate rates.")
("place.cost", "fullParagraph", "The rate cards show reported values from that software record. They are not new browser measurements or a universal optimum for real devices. A survival-conditioned view and the raw return answer different questions; the lost part must not disappear from the account.")
("place.cost", "fullParagraph", "The wider frontier asks for work, heat, precision, preparation, sampling and verification costs as well as a desired state. What would count as success if every impressive result quietly discarded its expense?")
("place.cost", "word", "word.model")
("place.cost", "pause", "What would make the explanations disagree?")
("place.cost", "instruction", "Choose a setting. A larger number means a slower loop here. Compare how much signal returns with how much is lost.")
