("place.cosmos.choice.0", "destination", "place.scale")
("place.cosmos.choice.0", "label", "Look between the scales")
("place.cosmos.choice.0", "kind", "Ideas to compare")
("place.cosmos", "choice", "place.cosmos.choice.1")
("place.cosmos.choice.1", "destination", "place.laboratory")
("place.cosmos.choice.1", "label", "Return to a measurable question")
("place.cosmos.choice.1", "kind", "Ideas to compare")
("place.cosmos", "choice", "place.cosmos.choice.2")
("place.cosmos.choice.2", "destination", "place.hiddenness")
("place.cosmos.choice.2", "label", "Enter an optional reading")
("place.cosmos.choice.2", "kind", "Optional theological crossing")
("place.scale", "isa", "Place")
("place.scale", "slug", "scale")
("place.scale", "route", "places/scale.html")
("place.scale", "title", "Zoom out. What disappears?")
("place.scale", "lead", "A wider view can keep the total while losing where the parts were.")
("place.scale", "region", "The changing scale")
("place.scale", "chapter", "33")
("place.scale", "source", "note.costs")
("place.scale", "light", "unseen")
("place.scale", "paragraph", "The small grid has eight filled cells. Group them into larger blocks. The total stays eight, but the view no longer shows the arrangement inside each block.")
("place.scale", "paragraph", "A summary can be useful without preserving every detail. The research asks a harder question: which rules and measurements remain reliable when the scale changes? The grid is an example of information loss, not a proof about physics.")
("place.scale", "fullParagraph", "The illustration groups a fixed set of small cells into larger blocks. Totals remain available while the arrangement within each block is hidden. The view changes resolution; the underlying small pattern does not magically reorganize itself.")
("place.scale", "fullParagraph", "The research programme asks a harder version: whether admissibility, dynamics and identifying records remain consistent across declared scale maps. A finite calculation does not supply a regulator-, volume- or time-uniform theorem.")
("place.scale", "fullParagraph", "Follow the same quantity across the views. Then name one distinction you can no longer recover. A macro description can be useful without retaining every microscopic answer.")
("place.scale", "word", "word.coarse-graining")
("place.scale", "pause", "How far does this explanation reach?")
("place.scale", "instruction", "Combine the cells into bigger blocks. Look for what stays the same and what disappears.")
("place.scale", "choice", "place.scale.choice.0")
("place.scale.choice.0", "destination", "place.cosmos")
("place.scale.choice.0", "label", "Return to the larger claim")
("place.scale.choice.0", "kind", "Another way back")
("place.scale", "choice", "place.scale.choice.1")
("place.scale.choice.1", "destination", "place.erasure")
("place.scale.choice.1", "label", "Inspect the discarded distinction")
("place.scale.choice.1", "kind", "Ideas to compare")
("place.scale", "choice", "place.scale.choice.2")
("place.scale.choice.2", "destination", "place.attention")
("place.scale.choice.2", "label", "Change another kind of view")
("place.scale.choice.2", "kind", "Ideas to compare")
("place.coexistence", "isa", "Place")
("place.coexistence", "slug", "coexistence")
("place.coexistence", "route", "places/coexistence.html")
("place.coexistence", "title", "What changes when time is short?")
("place.coexistence", "lead", "Waiting has different costs when needs are more urgent.")
("place.coexistence", "region", "The crossing of paths")
("place.coexistence", "chapter", "34")
("place.coexistence", "source", "note.scenarios")
("place.coexistence", "light", "boundary")
("place.coexistence", "paragraph", "An earlier Worldwright project used an invented computer model to compare responses to a shared problem. In its reported tests, the overall ranking changed when the model’s urgency changed.")
("place.coexistence", "paragraph", "Open each part of the result. Every tested case still involved tradeoffs. The model has not been shown to predict real communities, so its rankings do not tell us what people should do.")
("place.coexistence", "fullParagraph", "The older Worldwright research programme studies authored synthetic interventions. Its landscape reports a conditional finding: with delivery failures disabled, the lower-urgency cells favor mediation in an aggregate diagnostic, while the higher-urgency cells favor immediate visible action.")
("place.coexistence", "fullParagraph", "The same source retains Pareto tradeoffs in every cell. A winning aggregate does not mean every coordinate improves. The mechanisms and their real-world calibration remain unidentified, so these results do not authorize humanitarian policy or explain a person’s circumstances.")
("place.coexistence", "fullParagraph", "Choose which part of the reported model result to inspect. The encounter changes the reading of an existing synthetic record; it does not rerun the model or tell you what a real community should do.")
("place.coexistence", "word", "word.synthetic model")
("place.coexistence", "word", "word.Pareto tradeoff")
("place.coexistence", "pause", "Who lives with the consequences?")
("place.coexistence", "instruction", "Open each part of the computer result. Keep its limits beside its ranking.")
("place.coexistence", "choice", "place.coexistence.choice.0")
("place.coexistence.choice.0", "destination", "place.weights")
("place.coexistence.choice.0", "label", "See how the overall score is made")
("place.coexistence.choice.0", "kind", "Ideas to compare")
("place.coexistence", "choice", "place.coexistence.choice.1")
("place.coexistence.choice.1", "destination", "place.care")
("place.coexistence.choice.1", "label", "Return to particular needs")
("place.coexistence.choice.1", "kind", "Ideas to compare")
("place.coexistence", "choice", "place.coexistence.choice.2")
("place.coexistence.choice.2", "destination", "place.commons")
("place.coexistence.choice.2", "label", "Keep the uncertainty available")
("place.coexistence.choice.2", "kind", "Ideas to compare")
("place.weights", "isa", "Place")
("place.weights", "slug", "weights")
("place.weights", "route", "places/weights.html")
("place.weights", "title", "Who decides what matters?")
("place.weights", "lead", "A score can change when we change what matters most.")
("place.weights", "region", "The weight of a choice")
("place.weights", "chapter", "35")
("place.weights", "source", "note.scenarios")
("place.weights", "light", "boundary")
("place.weights", "paragraph", "Imagine two plans. In this made-up example, A scores 8 for speed and 2 for thoroughness. B has the opposite scores. Move the slider to change how much speed should count.")
("place.weights", "paragraph", "The options stay the same while their ranking changes. The numbers are invented and describe no people. The example makes one question visible: who chose what the score should value?")
("place.weights", "fullParagraph", "Two toy options sit on this desk. One has more of the first quantity and less of the second; the other reverses that arrangement. Move the declared weight and watch the arithmetic ranking change. Neither option has changed its two coordinates.")
("place.weights", "fullParagraph", "These numbers are invented for the illustration and describe no people, outcomes or source dataset. Their role is to make aggregation inspectable. In the older Worldwright model, the landscape likewise distinguishes a diagnostic aggregate from moral or political authority.")
("place.weights", "fullParagraph", "A responsible account names its objective, whose judgment supplied it and which costs the score can conceal. It can also retain the alternatives when there is no agreed single ranking.")
("place.weights", "word", "word.weight")
("place.weights", "word", "word.tradeoff")
("place.weights", "pause", "Who lives with the consequences?")
("place.weights", "instruction", "Move the slider to change what the score values. The options themselves stay the same.")
("place.weights", "choice", "place.weights.choice.0")
("place.weights.choice.0", "destination", "place.coexistence")
("place.weights.choice.0", "label", "Return to the conditional result")
("place.weights.choice.0", "kind", "Another way back")
("place.weights", "choice", "place.weights.choice.1")
("place.weights.choice.1", "destination", "place.permission")
("place.weights.choice.1", "label", "Separate capability from responsibility")
("place.weights.choice.1", "kind", "Ideas to compare")
("place.weights", "choice", "place.weights.choice.2")
("place.weights.choice.2", "destination", "place.reachable")
("place.weights.choice.2", "label", "Ask whether the preferred option is reachable")
("place.weights.choice.2", "kind", "Ideas to compare")
("place.hiddenness", "isa", "Place")
("place.hiddenness", "slug", "hiddenness")
("place.hiddenness", "route", "places/hiddenness.html")
("place.hiddenness", "title", "Room for faith. Room for a question.")
("place.hiddenness", "lead", "This path explores a religious interpretation with its starting beliefs made clear.")
("place.hiddenness", "region", "The quiet horizon")
("place.hiddenness", "chapter", "36")
("place.hiddenness", "source", "note.meanings")
("place.hiddenness", "light", "boundary")
("place.hiddenness", "paragraph", "The source begins by assuming God exists. It then asks how a good God might make room for a world in which people have real choices.")
("place.hiddenness", "paragraph", "That reading does not explain away every case of suffering or absence. You can stay with the question, follow the Christian account of hope, or return to the scientific paths. They do not require this belief.")
("place.hiddenness", "fullParagraph", "The optional synthesis assumes that God exists, then adds claims about unsurpassable goodness, the integrity of creation and kenotic action. Those premises support a possible reading of a lawful world. They are not conclusions extracted from a detector or an algebra.")
("place.hiddenness", "fullParagraph", "Within that reading, making room for creaturely agency need not mean abandonment. Yet the account refuses to infer that every instance of hiddenness or non-intervention is justified. A general picture cannot settle the suffering of a particular person.")
("place.hiddenness", "fullParagraph", "You may stay with the question, continue into an explicitly Christian account of hope, or return to the scientific observatory. None of the scientific paths requires passing through this horizon.")
("place.hiddenness", "word", "word.theology")
("place.hiddenness", "word", "word.assumption")
("place.hiddenness", "pause", "What may we hope for?")
("place.hiddenness", "choice", "place.hiddenness.choice.0")
("place.hiddenness.choice.0", "destination", "place.hope")
("place.hiddenness.choice.0", "label", "Continue into the Christian reading")
("place.hiddenness.choice.0", "kind", "Optional continuation")
("place.hiddenness", "choice", "place.hiddenness.choice.1")
("place.hiddenness.choice.1", "destination", "place.texts")
("place.hiddenness.choice.1", "label", "Visit the reading room")
("place.hiddenness.choice.1", "kind", "Ideas to compare")
("place.hiddenness", "choice", "place.hiddenness.choice.2")
("place.hiddenness.choice.2", "destination", "place.cosmos")
("place.hiddenness.choice.2", "label", "Return to the observatory")
("place.hiddenness.choice.2", "kind", "Another way back")
("place.hope", "isa", "Place")
("place.hope", "slug", "hope")
("place.hope", "route", "places/hope.html")
("place.hope", "title", "Restoration without erasure.")
("place.hope", "lead", "Hope for restoration does not require calling suffering good.")
("place.hope", "region", "The light beyond")
("place.hope", "chapter", "37")
("place.hope", "source", "note.meanings")
("place.hope", "light", "boundary")
("place.hope", "paragraph", "In the source’s Christian reading, God enters human life and shares its cost. Resurrection names a hope that brokenness will not have the final word.")
("place.hope", "paragraph", "This is a statement of faith, not a result from physics. It does not ask you to say that your own losses were necessary or deserved. It offers a question about how hope might shape care, patience and honesty.")
("place.hope", "fullParagraph", "The synthesis’s Christian continuation speaks of incarnation as entering creaturely conditions, the cross as sharing their cost without domination, and resurrection as restoration beyond a broken present. These are theological commitments, not a physical mechanism or a forecast produced by this site.")
("place.hope", "fullParagraph", "Its image of restoration preserves the reality of the history being restored. The picture does not establish that a loss was necessary, deserved or beneficial. No visitor is asked to reinterpret their own suffering to fit it.")
("place.hope", "fullParagraph", "What might such hope ask of an inquirer? Patience, truthfulness, care with other people’s costs, hospitality to correction and the willingness to leave useful work for someone else. Those are invitations to practice, not evidence that the underlying assumptions have been proved.")
("place.hope", "word", "word.incarnation")
("place.hope", "word", "word.resurrection")
("place.hope", "pause", "What may we hope for?")
("place.hope", "choice", "place.hope.choice.0")
("place.hope.choice.0", "destination", "place.hiddenness")
("place.hope.choice.0", "label", "Return to the unresolved question")
("place.hope.choice.0", "kind", "Another way back")
("place.hope", "choice", "place.hope.choice.1")
("place.hope.choice.1", "destination", "place.care")
("place.hope.choice.1", "label", "Bring hope back to attention")
("place.hope.choice.1", "kind", "Ideas to compare")
("place.hope", "choice", "place.hope.choice.2")
("place.hope.choice.2", "destination", "place.commons")
("place.hope.choice.2", "label", "Leave useful work for another")
("place.hope.choice.2", "kind", "Ideas to compare")
("place.texts", "isa", "Place")
("place.texts", "slug", "texts")
("place.texts", "route", "places/texts.html")
("place.texts", "title", "A reading is not yet a prediction.")
("place.texts", "lead", "A reading that fits a text is not yet evidence that it predicted an event.")
("place.texts", "region", "The reading room")
("place.texts", "chapter", "38")
("place.texts", "source", "note.texts")
("place.texts", "light", "boundary")
("place.texts", "paragraph", "Start with the passage, when it was written and what it claims. Then ask what separate evidence there is for the event it describes.")
("place.texts", "paragraph", "The research keeps these questions apart. It also asks whether an interpretation works on cases that were not used to invent it. This room makes no forecast and gives no personal prophecy.")
("place.texts", "fullParagraph", "The prophetic-admissibility pilot distinguishes literary fit, a narrative’s own account of fulfilment, independently dated outcomes, held-out validation and theological attribution. Each is a different claim. An exact calculation at one rung cannot silently promote the reading to the next.")
("place.texts", "fullParagraph", "The catalogue deliberately includes uncertain candidacies and names traditions still outside its coverage. Membership means a passage deserves examination as future-directed language; it does not establish supernatural truth, fulfilment or predictive reliability.")
("place.texts", "fullParagraph", "Inspect the evidence questions on the desk. There are no dates to optimize toward, no forecast of your future and no personal reading. The aim is a disciplined encounter with interpretation and its alternatives.")
("place.texts", "word", "word.held-out test")
("place.texts", "pause", "What may we hope for?")
("place.texts", "instruction", "Open each kind of evidence. Notice why one does not automatically establish the next.")
("place.texts", "choice", "place.texts.choice.0")
("place.texts.choice.0", "destination", "place.hiddenness")
("place.texts.choice.0", "label", "Return to the optional horizon")
("place.texts.choice.0", "kind", "Another way back")
("place.texts", "choice", "place.texts.choice.1")
("place.texts.choice.1", "destination", "place.preservation")
("place.texts.choice.1", "label", "Think about a surviving witness")
("place.texts.choice.1", "kind", "Ideas to compare")
("place.texts", "choice", "place.texts.choice.2")
("place.texts.choice.2", "destination", "place.closedpath")
("place.texts.choice.2", "label", "Ask what a failed reading teaches")
("place.texts.choice.2", "kind", "Ideas to compare")
("place.commons", "isa", "Place")
("place.commons", "slug", "commons")
("place.commons", "route", "places/commons.html")
("place.commons", "title", "Someone else can change the question.")
("place.commons", "lead", "An outside reply can reveal a question we failed to ask ourselves.")
("place.commons", "region", "The unfinished commons")
("place.commons", "chapter", "39")
("place.commons", "source", "note.correspondence")
("place.commons", "light", "boundary")
("place.commons", "paragraph", "The correspondence record keeps an earlier assessment and the response that changed the discussion. It identifies whose view is whose. An exchange does not become agreement or independent verification.")
("place.commons", "paragraph", "Open the cards to see what helps another person check a contribution: the exact source, what was expected, what happened and what could be tried next. Nothing is sent from this page.")
("place.commons", "fullParagraph", "The correspondence lane retains exchanges as attributed history. In the OPH conversation, an external reply exposed a reciprocal burden: a corpus asking another programme about consciousness must also say where its own account stops.")
("place.commons", "fullParagraph", "The record preserves the initial assessment and the later response rather than making the first silently agree with the second. It does not turn the exchange into a joint paper, peer review or endorsement. The voices remain separately attributed.")
("place.commons", "fullParagraph", "Open the cards for what a useful contribution can carry: an exact question, the relevant source and version, what was expected, what happened, and the narrow conclusion. The site sends nothing on your behalf. A contribution begins as a proposal another person can inspect.")
("place.commons", "word", "word.independent verification")
("place.commons", "word", "word.evidence family")
("place.commons", "pause", "What can we leave for one another?")
("place.commons", "instruction", "Open the cards to see what helps someone else check a contribution.")
("place.commons", "supplement", "note.contribution")
("place.commons", "choice", "place.commons.choice.0")
("place.commons.choice.0", "destination", "place.closedpath")
("place.commons.choice.0", "label", "Leave the failed route visible")
("place.commons.choice.0", "kind", "Ideas to compare")
("place.commons", "choice", "place.commons.choice.1")
("place.commons.choice.1", "destination", "place.workshop")
("place.commons.choice.1", "label", "Try one bounded change")
("place.commons.choice.1", "kind", "Ideas to compare")
("place.commons", "choice", "place.commons.choice.2")
("place.commons.choice.2", "destination", "place.attention")
("place.commons.choice.2", "label", "Listen for the question you missed")
("place.commons.choice.2", "kind", "Ideas to compare")
("place.closedpath", "isa", "Place")
("place.closedpath", "slug", "closedpath")
("place.closedpath", "route", "places/closedpath.html")
("place.closedpath", "title", "A failed attempt can still help.")
("place.closedpath", "lead", "A failed attempt is useful when we can explain exactly what it rules out.")
("place.closedpath", "region", "The retained refusal")
("place.closedpath", "chapter", "40")
("place.closedpath", "source", "note.negatives")
("place.closedpath", "light", "boundary")
("place.closedpath", "paragraph", "Did the program break? Did a route fail under certain conditions? Was something proved impossible within a model? Or did the test simply lack enough information?")
("place.closedpath", "paragraph", "Choose a kind of failure. Each leaves different next steps available. Keeping those limits clear can save another person from repeating the same mistake.")
("place.closedpath", "fullParagraph", "The negative-results ledger distinguishes a software defect, an obstruction under stated assumptions, an exact no-go result and a test that lacked identifying information. “It failed” is too small a description to tell the next person what remains possible.")
("place.closedpath", "fullParagraph", "Each admitted negative record names the claim, adequacy of the test, active domain, evidence, allowed inference and stronger inference refused. It also names what new datum or changed assumption could reopen the route.")
("place.closedpath", "fullParagraph", "Examine a refusal here as a research object. Keeping a failed promotion visible can protect a correct smaller result from being discarded with it. Truthful limits make the world larger by showing where a different question can begin.")
("place.closedpath", "word", "word.no-go result")
("place.closedpath", "word", "word.scope")
("place.closedpath", "pause", "What can we leave for one another?")
("place.closedpath", "instruction", "Choose a kind of failure. Read what it rules out and what it leaves open.")
("place.closedpath", "choice", "place.closedpath.choice.0")
("place.closedpath.choice.0", "destination", "place.commons")
("place.closedpath.choice.0", "label", "Leave something others can check")
("place.closedpath.choice.0", "kind", "Another way back")
("place.closedpath", "choice", "place.closedpath.choice.1")
("place.closedpath.choice.1", "destination", "place.code")
("place.closedpath.choice.1", "label", "Return to the mathematical comparison")
("place.closedpath.choice.1", "kind", "Ideas to compare")
("place.closedpath", "choice", "place.closedpath.choice.2")
("place.closedpath.choice.2", "destination", "place.possibility")
("place.closedpath.choice.2", "label", "See what remains possible")
("place.closedpath.choice.2", "kind", "Ideas to compare")
("note.reflection", "isa", "SourceNote")
("note.reflection", "title", "Scenes for reflection")
("note.reflection", "originalTitle", "Reader recognition, truth and the water")
("note.reflection", "kind", "Authored reflective scene · composition proposal")
("note.reflection", "note", "These fictional encounters develop Aaron’s invitation to address the reader, allow humour and use the horse-and-water reflection image. They are assistant-authored composition proposals, not additional autobiography, psychological findings or advice about a particular relationship.")
("note.reflection", "paragraph", "An invented scene for reflection")
("note.reflection", "paragraph", "These scenes develop Aaron’s ideas about helping a reader recognize something for themselves. They do not add to his life story or assess the reader.")
("note.reflection", "href", "retained/library/reflection.txt")
("note.frontier", "isa", "SourceNote")
("note.frontier", "title", "What measurements miss")
("note.frontier", "originalTitle", "The admissibility frontier")
("note.frontier", "kind", "Current research synthesis")
("note.frontier", "note", "Experiments can erase phase, delay, boundary order, orientation and correlations. The programme asks which distinguishing records an apparatus could preserve. The physical identification of the proposed structures remains open.")
("note.frontier", "paragraph", "An overview of the current research")
("note.frontier", "paragraph", "Asks which differences our measurements leave out and how another measurement might reveal them. It does not identify a new physical theory as true.")
("note.frontier", "href", "retained/library/frontier.txt")
("note.invariant", "isa", "SourceNote")
("note.invariant", "title", "What stays comparable along a path")
("note.invariant", "originalTitle", "The locally invariant carrier and its mirror face")
("note.invariant", "kind", "Exact mathematical construction")
("note.invariant", "note", "Pointwise invariance, compatible transport and trivial global return are separate claims. A loop can preserve the comparison structure and still return the transported state changed. Physical carrier identification remains open.")
("note.invariant", "paragraph", "A mathematical result; the physical connection is still open")
("note.invariant", "paragraph", "Explains how a comparison can remain valid during a journey even when the final state changes. This does not identify the material system that nature uses.")
("note.invariant", "href", "retained/library/invariant.txt")
("note.detector", "isa", "SourceNote")
("note.detector", "title", "What a measuring tool can see")
("note.detector", "originalTitle", "Scalar impedance and dark holonomy")
("note.detector", "kind", "Finite model result")
("note.detector", "note", "In the finite witness, the scalar port e₄ is orthogonal to the moving dark plane. Its response is independent of the loop parameter. Full matrix residues recover the projector; a calibrated sufficiently ranked port set is needed experimentally.")
("note.detector", "paragraph", "A result in a specified mathematical model")
("note.detector", "paragraph", "One reading can miss a changing part of the model. Several well-chosen, checked measurements can reveal it. This is not yet a physical measurement.")
("note.detector", "href", "retained/library/detector.txt")
("note.instrument", "isa", "SourceNote")
("note.instrument", "title", "The recorded computer experiment")
("note.instrument", "originalTitle", "The four-mode virtual instrument")
("note.instrument", "kind", "Documented virtual experiment")
("note.instrument", "note", "The corpus documents a deterministic 24-run virtual experiment and replay certificate. Real-time I/O and physical hardware remain external. This website provides an explanatory illustration, not a replay of those calculations.")
("note.instrument", "paragraph", "A recorded computer experiment")
("note.instrument", "paragraph", "The source keeps a set of computer runs and a program for checking them. Real equipment and physical measurements are separate work.")
("note.instrument", "href", "retained/library/instrument.txt")
("note.contact", "isa", "SourceNote")
("note.contact", "title", "When an answer misses the question")
("note.contact", "originalTitle", "From felt mismatch to inspectable structure")
("note.contact", "kind", "Applied epistemology proposal")
("note.contact", "note", "The inquiry begins with a recognition that a response missed the point. It develops contrast cases and rival explanations, with prediction on fresh examples as the next test. It does not establish a universal model of tacit knowledge.")
("note.contact", "paragraph", "A proposal developed from one recorded mistake")
("note.contact", "paragraph", "Studies how an answer missed the speaker’s question. Its suggested checks still need testing in fresh cases.")
("note.contact", "href", "retained/library/contact.txt")
("note.correction", "isa", "SourceNote")
("note.correction", "title", "The withdrawn explanation")
("note.correction", "originalTitle", "The withdrawn cross-context interpretation")
("note.correction", "kind", "Retained correction")
("note.correction", "note", "Withdrawn on 19 October 2026 for importing an unrelated physics programme into the illegible-knowledge inquiry. Its proposed interpretation should not be treated as an established bridge.")
("note.correction", "paragraph", "An earlier interpretation that was withdrawn")
("note.correction", "paragraph", "The interpretation brought one research framework into a different question. It is kept so readers can see the mistake and the correction.")
("note.correction", "href", "retained/library/correction.txt")
("note.kenosis", "isa", "SourceNote")
("note.kenosis", "title", "Power, restraint and responsibility")
("note.kenosis", "originalTitle", "Kenotic governance and shared responsibility")
("note.kenosis", "kind", "Theological and philosophical argument")
("note.kenosis", "note", "Capability, moral permission and an obligation to exercise power are distinguished. Restraint must also be examined where one agent destroys another’s effective agency. The argument remains defeasible and is not a physical derivation.")
("note.kenosis", "paragraph", "A philosophical and theological argument")
("note.kenosis", "paragraph", "Distinguishes the ability to use power from permission and responsibility. It makes room for both restraint and protection. It is not a scientific law.")
("note.kenosis", "href", "retained/library/kenosis.txt")
("note.sourcery", "isa", "SourceNote")
("note.sourcery", "title", "Software that keeps its workings visible")
("note.sourcery", "originalTitle", "Programme algebra")
("note.sourcery", "kind", "Software and representation programme")
("note.sourcery", "note", "The science programme is expressed through the compositional grammar used by Sourcery. Typed objects preserve assumptions, evidence and refused transformations. A complete protocol does not establish that an experiment ran.")
("note.sourcery", "paragraph", "A software research programme")
("note.sourcery", "paragraph", "Explores how software descriptions can keep sources, assumptions and rejected changes visible. A complete description does not prove an experiment ran.")
("note.sourcery", "href", "retained/library/sourcery.txt")
("note.monster", "isa", "SourceNote")
("note.monster", "title", "Three mathematical structures compared")
("note.monster", "originalTitle", "The rank-24 detector ladder")
("note.monster", "kind", "Exact within a declared candidate family")
("note.monster", "note", "E₈³, Leech and Moonshine candidates share a coarse thermal signature. Their weight-one current dimensions are 744, 24 and 0. The three-way distinction is not an unrestricted uniqueness theorem or evidence that a physical system realizes the Monster.")
("note.monster", "paragraph", "A mathematical comparison of three chosen structures")
("note.monster", "paragraph", "A count distinguishes three named mathematical structures. It is not a proof about every possible structure or evidence of a physical Monster system.")
("note.monster", "href", "retained/library/monster.txt")
("note.personal", "isa", "SourceNote")
("note.personal", "title", "About Aaron’s personal account")
("note.personal", "originalTitle", "Aaron’s personal account")
("note.personal", "kind", "Author account · working draft")
("note.personal", "note", "Adapted from Aaron’s account in the originating conversation. These passages express his experience. They do not independently establish another family member’s perspective, intentions or a causal explanation of the family’s experience. Wording remains open to his revision.")
("note.personal", "paragraph", "Aaron’s account, in wording open to revision")
("note.personal", "paragraph", "This passage expresses Aaron’s experience. It does not establish what another family member thought or intended.")
("note.authorship", "isa", "SourceNote")
("note.authorship", "title", "People choosing; machines helping")
("note.authorship", "originalTitle", "Human authorship, assisted by AI")
("note.authorship", "kind", "Product philosophy · inspectable example")
("note.authorship", "note", "Developed with Aaron in the Encounter discussion: remove unnecessary labour, preserve human authorship, and make consequences and evidence inspectable. Autonomy, human taste and risk-appropriate assurance are design commitments. The accompanying example executes a bounded typed edit through the existing Temple kernel. Its checks do not establish independent verification, a complete authoring environment, employment outcomes or a measured productivity multiplier.")
("note.authorship", "paragraph", "A design philosophy with a small working example")
("note.authorship", "paragraph", "The workshop lets you edit two values and follow the results through the existing checking system. It is not a complete authoring environment or independent verification.")
("note.contribution", "isa", "SourceNote")
("note.contribution", "title", "How to offer a checkable contribution")
("note.contribution", "originalTitle", "Context Hub contribution contract")
("note.contribution", "kind", "Contribution proposal contract · no admission effect")
("note.contribution", "note", "Defines bounded attributable contributions, exact sources, evidence-family identity and a narrow conclusion. Inbox acceptance has no scientific or runtime admission effect. The reader does not submit a contribution or contact anyone.")
("note.contribution", "paragraph", "Guidance for offering a checkable contribution")
("note.contribution", "paragraph", "A useful proposal names its question, source and limits. Receiving a proposal does not make its claims true or authorize a system change.")
("note.contribution", "href", "retained/library/contribution.txt")
("note.possibilities", "isa", "SourceNote")
("note.possibilities", "title", "What is possible, allowed and reachable")
("note.possibilities", "originalTitle", "Dynatology: ontology and scope")
("note.possibilities", "kind", "Investigation framework · declared distinctions")
("note.possibilities", "note", "Distinguishes represented possibility, admissibility, viability, reachability, selection, realization and observation. This is a framework for investigations, not a physical theory. The garden is a small authored compatibility example.")
("note.possibilities", "paragraph", "A framework for asking research questions")
("note.possibilities", "paragraph", "Keeps “possible,” “allowed,” “reachable” and “observed” separate. The garden is a small example with visible rules.")
("note.possibilities", "href", "retained/library/possibilities.txt")
("note.memory", "isa", "SourceNote")
("note.memory", "title", "How the past can keep affecting a system")
("note.memory", "originalTitle", "Hidden elimination and the quadratic memory theorem")
("note.memory", "kind", "Exact finite-dimensional linear result")
("note.memory", "note", "Eliminating coupled hidden coordinates can produce a memory kernel and a term depending on their initial state. Autonomous closure requires an invariant detector kernel. The browser illustration is a two-coordinate Euler example, not a replay of the corpus certificate.")
("note.memory", "paragraph", "A mathematical result for specified linked quantities")
("note.memory", "paragraph", "Removing a quantity from an equation can leave an effect that depends on the past. The browser uses a smaller example to explain the idea.")
("note.memory", "href", "retained/library/memory.txt")
("note.erasure", "isa", "SourceNote")
("note.erasure", "title", "What a record cannot recover")
("note.erasure", "originalTitle", "No universal scar and the recovery boundary")
("note.erasure", "kind", "Exact information boundary · scoped recovery")
("note.erasure", "note", "States in the same detector fibre cannot be distinguished using only functions of that retained record. Recovery requires an additional distinguishing record or a richer theory. The dot-and-sum example is an authored finite illustration.")
("note.erasure", "paragraph", "A mathematical limit on what a record can tell us")
("note.erasure", "paragraph", "If two origins leave exactly the same record, that record alone cannot tell them apart. A second, different record can help.")
("note.erasure", "href", "retained/library/erasure.txt")
("note.mind", "isa", "SourceNote")
("note.mind", "title", "Attention, awareness, choice and action")
("note.mind", "originalTitle", "Attention, will and conscious access")
("note.mind", "kind", "Literature-grounded functional atlas · candidate joins")
("note.mind", "note", "Separates readiness, selection, conscious access, control, action and agency in distributed systems. Candidate biological joins retain their uncertainty. The canopy is an illustration of changing a view, not an attention test or a model of your brain.")
("note.mind", "paragraph", "A research overview with uncertain connections marked")
("note.mind", "paragraph", "Separates attention, awareness, choosing and acting. The drawings do not test the reader or provide a completed explanation of consciousness.")
("note.mind", "href", "retained/library/mind.txt")
("note.relations", "isa", "SourceNote")
("note.relations", "title", "What an arrow needs to explain")
("note.relations", "originalTitle", "Brain relationship algebra")
("note.relations", "kind", "Typed relationship proposal")
("note.relations", "note", "Makes conditional, negative and unresolved relationships explicit. A link needs its inputs and evidence; sequential, parallel and alternative compositions have different meanings. The intention scene is conceptual, not a diagnosis or a new neuroscience finding.")
("note.relations", "paragraph", "A proposal for describing connections carefully")
("note.relations", "paragraph", "Names what a connection needs and what evidence supports it. A drawn arrow is not proof that the connection exists in a brain.")
("note.relations", "href", "retained/library/relations.txt")
("note.material", "isa", "SourceNote")
("note.material", "title", "Investigating materials and their history")
("note.material", "originalTitle", "Physics to archaeology through the pyramids")
("note.material", "kind", "Proposed material-history decomposition")
("note.material", "note", "Connects named materials, transformations, preservation, measurements and historical inference while keeping their evidence separate. It does not derive archaeology from a symmetry group. The browser stone is illustrative and represents no sampled object.")
("note.material", "paragraph", "A proposed approach to material and historical evidence")
("note.material", "paragraph", "Connects materials, making, surviving traces and historical context. The drawing is not a measured archaeological sample.")
("note.material", "href", "retained/library/material.txt")
("note.patterns", "isa", "SourceNote")
("note.patterns", "title", "The exact counting relationship")
("note.patterns", "originalTitle", "Girih inflation and the index-two scar")
("note.patterns", "kind", "Exact transformation bridge · carrier identification open")
("note.patterns", "note", "The two-state matrix [[2,1],[1,1]] is exact in the declared Fibonacci/Penrose count action. The published girih construction reaches a related iterate through additional operations. The displayed counts do not reconstruct a historical tiling or establish a physical carrier.")
("note.patterns", "paragraph", "An exact counting relationship with limits")
("note.patterns", "paragraph", "Connects specific pattern rules through explicit mathematical steps. Similar numbers or shapes do not by themselves establish the same material or history.")
("note.patterns", "href", "retained/library/patterns.txt")
("note.codes", "isa", "SourceNote")
("note.codes", "title", "Checking a larger error-correcting code")
("note.codes", "originalTitle", "Golden–Golay integral encoder audit")
("note.codes", "kind", "Exact arithmetic audit · retained obstruction")
("note.codes", "note", "Recovers a bounded encoder but rejects an intrinsic equivariant ordering. The browser uses a separately declared three-bit repetition code to explain correction radius. It is not the ternary Golay code, a biological mechanism or a physical discovery.")
("note.codes", "paragraph", "A mathematical check that also rules out one proposed step")
("note.codes", "paragraph", "The source studies a larger code and a proposed ordering rule. The browser’s three-bit example is different and has its own small error limit.")
("note.codes", "href", "retained/library/codes.txt")
("note.laboratory", "isa", "SourceNote")
("note.laboratory", "title", "Plans for physical tests")
("note.laboratory", "originalTitle", "The finesse technology pathway")
("note.laboratory", "kind", "Experimental roadmap · proposed physical work")
("note.laboratory", "note", "Proposes controlled carriers, references, coherent records, nuisance checks and cross-carrier replication. Useful sensing or system-identification improvements can remain even if all anomalies receive conventional explanations. The bench is a design exercise, not acquired data.")
("note.laboratory", "paragraph", "A plan for physical tests")
("note.laboratory", "paragraph", "Describes possible equipment, comparisons and records. The browser does not collect experimental measurements.")
("note.laboratory", "href", "retained/library/laboratory.txt")
("note.costs", "isa", "SourceNote")
("note.costs", "title", "Costs and limits of a result")
("note.costs", "originalTitle", "Cost, backaction, scale and rare events")
("note.costs", "kind", "Process-frontier census · open research lanes")
("note.costs", "note", "Separates convergence from resource cost, conditioned trajectories from unconditional channels, and finite calculations from scale-uniform control. The rate table in the reader comes from the documented four-mode virtual experiment; it is numerical evidence in that authored model.")
("note.costs", "paragraph", "A map of research questions about costs and limits")
("note.costs", "paragraph", "Asks what a result costs, what measurement changes, and which conclusions survive a change of scale. Many questions remain open.")
("note.costs", "href", "retained/library/costs.txt")
("note.cosmos", "isa", "SourceNote")
("note.cosmos", "title", "Open questions about the universe")
("note.cosmos", "originalTitle", "Frontier physics target set")
("note.cosmos", "kind", "Maintained research target map · open gates")
("note.cosmos", "note", "The target map keeps physical identification, coefficient selection, common-parameter cosmology and continuum limits as distinct gates. A target or workflow priority is not confirmation of new physics. The observatory offers questions, not simulated observations.")
("note.cosmos", "paragraph", "A map of open questions in physics")
("note.cosmos", "paragraph", "Names work needed on gravity, particles and the universe. A place on this map is not evidence that a proposed explanation is correct.")
("note.cosmos", "href", "retained/library/cosmos.txt")
("note.scenarios", "isa", "SourceNote")
("note.scenarios", "title", "Results from an invented social model")
("note.scenarios", "originalTitle", "Worldwright research: current landscape")
("note.scenarios", "kind", "Authored synthetic intervention model · empirical calibration open")
("note.scenarios", "note", "The older research model is distinct from this storytelling product. Its frozen cells expose urgency-dependent aggregate rankings and Pareto tradeoffs under declared assumptions. Its mechanisms remain empirically unidentified; no policy, personal advice or moral authority follows from the rankings.")
("note.scenarios", "paragraph", "Results in an invented model; real-world testing is still needed")
("note.scenarios", "paragraph", "The reported ranking changes with urgency and with the chosen score. The model has not been shown to predict real communities or decide what is morally right.")
("note.scenarios", "href", "retained/library/scenarios.txt")
("note.meanings", "isa", "SourceNote")
("note.meanings", "title", "Optional religious interpretations")
("note.meanings", "originalTitle", "Optional endpoints, mythic readings and theology")
("note.meanings", "kind", "Optional interpretation · explicit theological assumptions")
("note.meanings", "note", "The theological reading explicitly assumes God exists, with further assumptions about goodness, creation and kenotic action. The Christian continuation is identified as such. Neither mathematics nor detector limits establish these assumptions, and suffering is not thereby justified.")
("note.meanings", "paragraph", "An optional religious interpretation")
("note.meanings", "paragraph", "Begins by assuming God exists and identifies its Christian continuation. It does not use mathematics to prove those beliefs or justify a person’s suffering.")
("note.meanings", "href", "retained/library/meanings.txt")
("note.texts", "isa", "SourceNote")
("note.texts", "title", "Testing interpretations of texts")
("note.texts", "originalTitle", "Prophetic admissibility")
("note.texts", "kind", "Historical and theological inference pilot · prediction ranking unidentified")
("note.texts", "note", "Separates literary fit, narrative fulfilment, dating, independent outcomes, held-out validation and theological attribution. Catalogue membership does not establish fulfilment. The encounter generates no dates, forecasts or personal prophecy; the bundled source describes a provisional research programme with its own limits.")
("note.texts", "paragraph", "A proposed way to test interpretations of texts")
("note.texts", "paragraph", "Separates a reading that fits a passage from an independently supported prediction. The encounter generates no dates or personal prophecy.")
("note.texts", "href", "retained/library/texts.txt")
("note.correspondence", "isa", "SourceNote")
("note.correspondence", "title", "A recorded exchange of views")
("note.correspondence", "originalTitle", "OPH and the Admissibility Frontier")
("note.correspondence", "kind", "Attributed conversation · no joint endorsement")
("note.correspondence", "note", "Records an external reply and the corpus response, including a correction about the limits of its own consciousness account. Editorial summaries remain the corpus’s interpretation. The reader does not invent dialogue or present correspondence as peer review.")
("note.correspondence", "paragraph", "A record of an exchange, with views separately attributed")
("note.correspondence", "paragraph", "Keeps an earlier assessment and a response that changed the discussion. It does not imply agreement, peer review or endorsement.")
("note.correspondence", "href", "retained/library/correspondence.txt")
("note.negatives", "isa", "SourceNote")
("note.negatives", "title", "Failures and what could reopen a question")
("note.negatives", "originalTitle", "Negative results and reopening ledger")
("note.negatives", "kind", "Scoped negative knowledge · explicit reopening conditions")
("note.negatives", "note", "Separates software defects, domain-dependent obstructions, no-go results and inadequate information. A negative result retains its exact claim, assumptions, evidence and cheapest reopening datum. A failed test does not justify an unrestricted impossibility claim.")
("note.negatives", "paragraph", "A record of failures with their limits kept clear")
("note.negatives", "paragraph", "Distinguishes a broken program, a failed route, a proof of impossibility under conditions, and a test with too little information. It also asks what could change the conclusion.")
("note.negatives", "href", "retained/library/negatives.txt")
("word.phase", "meaning", "Where a repeating signal is in its cycle: for example, whether it is rising or falling at a given moment.")
("word.detector", "meaning", "A tool or method that turns something happening into a record we can inspect.")
("word.holonomy", "meaning", "A change left after following a closed path. Returning to the same location does not always return everything to the same state.")
("word.calibration", "meaning", "Checking how a measuring tool’s readings relate to a known reference.")
("word.model", "meaning", "A simplified description used to explore a question. A result in the model needs further evidence before it describes the real world.")
("word.kenosis", "meaning", "A Christian term often translated as “self-emptying.” Here it means using power while making room for others to act.")
("word.agency", "meaning", "A person’s ability to make choices and have a meaningful effect on what happens.")
("word.authorship", "meaning", "Having a meaningful say in what is made, why it is made and how it is changed.")
("word.replay", "meaning", "Repeating a calculation from saved settings so its result can be checked.")
("word.control", "meaning", "A comparison designed to test whether an apparent effect could have another explanation.")
("word.representation", "meaning", "A view of something, such as a map, diagram, set of numbers or piece of text. Every view keeps some features and leaves others out.")
("word.assumption", "meaning", "Something an explanation takes as a starting point. A conclusion depends on the assumptions it needs.")
("word.source", "meaning", "Where a statement or displayed value comes from: for example, a document, a record or a piece of code.")
("word.projection", "meaning", "A particular view of the same thing. In the workshop, one story can appear as a page, a menu link or a row.")
("word.overlay", "meaning", "A local change to a view. In the workshop, the menu can use its own label while the story keeps its title.")
("word.admissibility", "meaning", "Whether something meets the stated rules or constraints. It does not mean that it will happen or that we can reach it.")
("word.positive response", "meaning", "A response described by a number table that gives zero or above for every direction you test. Here, that puts a limit on the shared term c. The formula is available under the calculation details.")
("word.coupling", "meaning", "A link through which two parts of a model can affect one another.")
("word.record", "meaning", "The information that was kept. A record may leave out differences that were present in the original event.")
("word.conscious access", "meaning", "Information becoming available for such things as reporting or deliberately using it. Explaining this does not by itself explain all experience.")
("word.feedback", "meaning", "Information about what happened returning to a system and potentially changing what happens next.")
("word.provenance", "meaning", "Where something came from and the history of how it reached us.")
("word.preservation", "meaning", "The processes that allow traces to survive, or that change and damage them over time.")
("word.transformation", "meaning", "A stated rule for changing one state, number pair or arrangement into another.")
("word.carrier", "meaning", "The particular thing being described by a model: for example, a set of numbers, a physical device or a system of relations. Shared mathematics does not make two carriers the same thing.")
("word.code", "meaning", "A rule for representing a message so that some errors can be detected or repaired.")
("word.bit", "meaning", "One binary digit: zero or one.")
("word.cosmology", "meaning", "The study of the universe’s large-scale structure and history.")
("word.coarse-graining", "meaning", "Combining small details into larger groups. This can preserve a total while losing information about the parts.")
("word.synthetic model", "meaning", "An invented model for exploring assumptions. Its outcomes are not observations of real people or communities.")
("word.Pareto tradeoff", "meaning", "A choice where improving one quantity comes with a loss in another. A single overall score can hide that loss.")
("word.weight", "meaning", "How much a quantity contributes to a combined score.")
("word.tradeoff", "meaning", "Gaining something while giving up something else.")
("word.theology", "meaning", "Thinking and arguments about God and religious belief. These may begin from beliefs that the reader does not share.")
("word.incarnation", "meaning", "In Christian belief, God entering human life in Jesus.")
("word.resurrection", "meaning", "In Christian belief, rising from death. Here it is part of an identified account of hope, not a result from physics.")
("word.held-out test", "meaning", "Trying a rule on examples that were not used to develop or choose it.")
("word.independent verification", "meaning", "A check that is genuinely separate from the work being checked. Repeating a claim or summarizing the same run is not an independent check.")
("word.evidence family", "meaning", "Records that come from the same underlying event or test. Several versions of one result are not several independent confirmations.")
("word.no-go result", "meaning", "A proof that a proposed route cannot work under stated conditions. It does not rule out every differently stated route.")
("word.scope", "meaning", "The particular question, conditions and range to which a claim applies.")
("word.totalizing", "meaning", "Taking over so much attention that other things become hard to notice.")
